      "message": "Please fix this WARNING reported by autoreview issue finding: This is a great fix for the 32-bit rollover issue. The signed modulo arithmetic correctly handles wrap-arounds.\n\nHowever, consider a subtle race condition that this change might expose, related to the `TODO(b/213342294)` in `get_time()`.\n\nIf an event IRQ runs just after the 32-bit system timer rolls over, but before the system timer IRQ increments `clksrc_high`:\n1. `get_time()` will return a wrapped time (e.g. epoch `N`, `0x00000050`).\n2. `process_timers(0)` will incorrectly evaluate an expired timer at `N, 0xFFFFFFF0` as being in the future, and call `__hw_clock_event_set(0xFFFFFFF0)`. `evt_expired_us` becomes `0xFFFFFFF0`.\n3. Then, the system timer IRQ runs, increments `clksrc_high` to `N+1`, and calls `process_timers(1)`.\n4. The timer at `N, 0xFFFFFFF0` is now correctly expired. A new timer at `N+1, 0x00000100` becomes the next deadline.\n5. `__hw_clock_event_set(0x00000100)` is called.\n\nWith the new signed comparison: `(int32_t)(0x00000100 - 0xFFFFFFF0)` is `0x110` (positive). `__hw_clock_event_set` will incorrectly think the stale `0xFFFFFFF0` is an earlier deadline and `return` without updating the hardware timer! (The old unsigned comparison avoided this by pure luck, as `0x100 \u003c 0xFFFFFFF0`).\n\nTo make this fully robust against stale `evt_expired_us` values from a previous epoch, consider checking if `evt_expired_us` is already in the past:\n\n```c\n\tcurrent \u003d __hw_clock_source_read();\n\n\t/* Is deadline min value? (signed modulo delta) */\n\tif (evt_expired_us !\u003d 0 \u0026\u0026 \n\t    (int32_t)(evt_expired_us - current) \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026\n\t    (int32_t)(deadline - evt_expired_us) \u003e\u003d 0)\n\t\treturn;\n```",
